en box showing who is currently inside:\n   - Notice the patient in consultation (e.g. Token #101).\n   - Now look at the table right below it at **Patient #1 in line** (e.g. Token #102) and note their current predicted wait time (e.g. **`10 min`**) and expected time (e.g. **`10:30 AM`**).\n4. Click the yellow **Record Delay** button on the active consultation.\n5. In the pop-up box, type **`25`** and press **Enter** (this simulates that the consultation took 25 minutes longer than expected).\n6. **What to look for on screen:**\n   - A toast notification appears: *\"Consultation delayed by +25 mins! Recalculated all subsequent patient waiting times.\"*\n   - **Look at the table immediately:** Token #102's wait time just increased by 25 minutes (from 10 min $\\rightarrow$ **`35 min`**), and their expected consultation clock moved forward by 25 minutes!\n   - Every single patient queued behind them also had their clock adjusted forward.\n   *(This proves the dynamic cascading algorithm is actively calculating queue offsets in SQLite).*\n\n---\n\n### TEST 3: Verify Doctor Workload Rebalancing with Approval (Section 8 & 10)\n*Goal: Prove that the system identifies overloaded doctors, calculates time savings, and transfers patients upon admin approval.*\n\n1. In the sidebar, click **`5. Doctor Workload`**.\n2. Look at the chart and table:\n   - **Dr. Rajesh Sharma** is flagged with a **RED badge: OVERLOADED** with ~16 waiting patients.\n   - **Dr. Priya Nair** in the same department is **GREEN: OPTIMAL** with only ~3 waiting patients.\n3. Now click **`10. Reassignment`** in the sidebar.\n4. You will see an active decision card:\n   - **Problem:** *\"Dr. Rajesh Sharma is heavily loaded with 16 waiting patients, while Dr. Priya Nair has only 3.\"*\n   - **Recommended Action:** *\"Reassign 6 eligible Normal-priority patients from Dr. Rajesh Sharma to Dr. Priya Nair.\"*\n   - **Expected Benefit:** *\"Reduces Dr. Rajesh Sharma's queue wait by ~55 minutes.\"*\n5. Click the green button: **`APPROVE REASSIGNMENT`**.\n6. **What to look for on screen:**\n   - A success toast appears: *\"Successfully redistributed 6 patients and recalculated waiting times.\"*\n   - The status badge changes from `PENDING` to **`APPROVED`**.\n7. Now click back to **`3. Patient Queue`** or **`5. Doctor Workload`**:\n   - Dr. Rajesh Sharma's queue dropped from 16 down to **10 patients**.\n   - Dr. Priya Nair's queue gained the 6 patients.\n   - Both doctors' waiting times were recalculated in the database.\n\n---\n\n### TEST 4: Register a Live Patient & Watch Triage Priority (Section 2)\n*Goal: Prove live database insertion and emergency triage priority jumping.*\n\n1. In the sidebar, click **`2. Patient Management`**.\n2. Click the blue **`Register New Patient`** button at the top right.\n3. Fill in the form:\n   - **Name:** `Vikramaditya Rao`\n   - **Department:** `Pediatrics`\n   - **Priority:** Select **`Emergency (Immediate Intervention)`**\n   - **Clinical Reason:** `Fever-related Illness`\n4. Click **Register & Calculate Wait**.\n5. **What to look for on screen:**\n   - A toast notification confirms registration with a new Token number (e.g. `#176`).\n   - Look at the patient list: Vikramaditya Rao appears with a **RED Emergency pill**.\n   - Because they are an Emergency patient, the Random Forest model prioritized them, giving them an immediate consultation clock and minimal wait time ahead of normal patients!\n\n---\n\n### TEST 5: Verify Bed Shortage & 4-Hour Hourly Horizon (Section 7)\n*Goal: Prove that the hospital predicts ward saturation 1 to 4 hours before it happens.*\n\n1. In the sidebar, click **`7. Bed Occupancy`**.\n2. Look at the table row for **Cardiology**:\n   - It shows high occupancy (e.g. **`92%`**) and a **RED Critical badge**.\n   - Look at the forecast columns: **`+1 Hour`**, **`+2 Hours`**, **`+3 Hours`**, **`+4 Hours`**.\n   - The chart on the right shows the projected saturation curve.\n3. At the top right of the page, click the green button: **`Simulate Discharge (-1 Bed)`**.\n4. **What to look for on screen:**\n   - Cardiology occupancy immediately drops by 1 bed.\n   - The occupancy rate decreases, available beds increase, and the hourly forecast line shifts downward.\n5. Click **`Simulate Admission (+1 Bed)`** and watch the numbers rise back up.\n\n---\n\n### TEST 6: Disease Outbreak Spikes & Supply Depletion Alerts (Section 8 & 9)\n*Goal: Prove that clinical disease trends automatically trigger inventory stockout warnings.*\n\n1. In the sidebar, click **`8. Disease Trends`**.\n2. Review the 14-day chart showing Dengue, Malaria, and Influenza cases.\n3. Click the red button at the top right: **`Simulate Dengue Spike (+15)`**.\n4. A notification confirms that +15 verified and +24 suspected cases were injected into the system.\n5. Now click **`9. Inventory`** in the sidebar.\n6. **What to look for on screen:**\n   - Scroll to **`Dengue NS1 Antigen Rapid Test Kits`** and **`IV Normal Saline`**.\n   - They are flagged with a **RED badge: CRITICAL SHORTAGE RISK** because the spike in Dengue patients shortened the \"Days of Cover\" to less than 1.5 days!\n7. Click the green **`Restock`** button next to Dengue Test Kits, type **`200`**, and press Enter.\n   - The stock increases, and the badge recovers to **GREEN: NORMAL**.\n\n---\n\n### TEST 7: Interactive Simulation Lab (Section 12)\n*Goal: Use this during the hackathon to demonstrate quick \"What-If\" scenarios to judges.*\n\n1. Click **`12. Simulation Lab`** in the sidebar.\n2. Under **Patient Queue & Doctor Load Simulator**:\n   - Select any doctor from the dropdown.\n   - Click **`+10 Normal Patients`**: Watch the doctor's queue instantly surge.\n   - Click **`Toggle Doctor Break`**: The doctor is marked \"ON BREAK\" and patients' wait times adjust.\n   - Click **`Complete Current Consultation`**: The active patient is checked out, and the next patient is called.\n3. **Reset to Clean State:**\n   - When you are done playing with the simulation, click the red button: **`Reset Simulation to Baseline`**.\n   - The database instantly re-seeds itself back to the clean starting state!\n\n---\n\n### TEST 8: Machine Learning Performance Verification (Section 13)\n*Goal: Show the evaluator your actual machine learning accuracy and explainability.*\n\n1. Click **`13. ML Performance`** in the sidebar.\n2. Point out these genuine, calculated metrics to the evaluator:\n   - **Random Forest:** $R^2 \\approx 0.996$ (explains 99.6% of wait time variance), $\\text{MAE} \\approx 10\\text{ minutes}$.\n   - **Linear Regression Baseline:** $R^2 \\approx 0.860$, $\\text{MAE} \\approx 59.7\\text{ minutes}$.\n   - **Feature Importance Chart:** Explains that `patients_ahead` (42%) and `avg_consult_duration` (28%) have the greatest impact on waiting times.\n\n---\n\n### Summary Checklist to Show the Evaluator:\n| What to Show | How to Show It |\n|---|---|\n| **AI Wait Time Prediction** | Go to Section 4 $\\rightarrow$ Enter token `101` $\\rightarrow$ Show predicted clock & explainable inputs. |\n| **Dynamic Delay Recalculation** | Go to Section 3 $\\rightarrow$ Click `Record Delay` (+25m) $\\rightarrow$ Show all subsequent tokens shift forward. |\n| **Intelligent Rebalancing** | Go to Section 10 $\\rightarrow$ Click `Approve Reassignment` $\\rightarrow$ Show 6 patients move from Dr. Sharma to Dr. Nair. |\n| **Bed Shortage Forecasting** | Go to Section 7 $\\rightarrow$ Show Cardiology ward saturated at +3 hours. |\n| **Disease $\\rightarrow$ Inventory Link** | Go to Section 8 $\\rightarrow$ Simulate Dengue spike $\\rightarrow$ Show Section 9 trigger kit shortage alarm. |","thinking":"The goal is to create a step-by-step guide for a hackathon participant to verify end-to-end functionality. The guide needs to be actionable and cover all components: backend, database, machine learning model, and UI.\n\nFocusing on the user's need for a concrete guide, the initial approach will be to break down the verification into distinct stages, starting with the simplest, most observable element and working toward more complex interactions. The guide will include actions the user should perform along with expected results.\n\n\n"}
{"step_index":101,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-30T14:14:56Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nin test 1 waiting time prediction part what does the predicted waiting time and the expected consultation show and on what basis they are showing the time\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-10-30T19:44:56+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
{"step_index":102,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-30T14:14:56Z","input_tokens":2991,"cache_read_tokens":125457,"output_tokens":1996,"content":"Here is the clear explanation of what both terms mean, what they show on screen, and the exact mathematical and machine learning basis used to calculate them.\n\n---\n\n### 1. What Do They Show?\n\n| Field | What It Represents | Real-Life Hospital Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Predicted Waiting Time** | The **duration in minutes** the patient must wait from the current moment until their consultation begins. | If it shows **`45 mins`**, it means: *\"You have approximately 45 minutes left before the doctor calls you into the clinic.\"* |\n| **Expected Consultation Time** | The **exact clock time (timestamp)** when the patient can expect to walk through the clinic door. | If the current time is `07:30 PM` and the wait is 45 mins, it shows **`08:15 PM`**. The patient can go get coffee or step out and knows to return by `08:15 PM`. |\n\n---\n\n### 2. On What Basis Is the Time Calculated?\n\nInstead of using a naive static multiplication (like `people * 10 mins`), Queue Oracle uses a **Random Forest Machine Learning Model** trained on 1,500 historical patient visits, combined with **live queue state data from the database**.\n\nThe prediction is calculated using **7 key operational inputs**:\n\n```\nLive Queue State (SQLite) ──┐\nDoctor Speed & Availability ┼──▶ [Random Forest Regressor] ──▶ Predicted Waiting Time (Minutes)\nTriage Priority Level     ──┘         (120 Decision Trees)               │\n                                                                         ▼\n                                                           + Current Clock Time\n                                                                         │\n                                                                         ▼\n                                                            Expected Consultation Time (e.g. 08:15 PM)\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. The 7 Features Fed into the Calculation:\n\n#### ① `patients_ahead` (Queue Position) — *42% Model Weight*\n- The model queries how many active patients are in line ahead of this specific token for their assigned doctor.\n- **Example:** If Token #108 has 4 patients ahead, the baseline wait starts around $4 \\times \\text{Doctor Speed}$.\n\n#### ② `avg_consult_duration` (Doctor Speed by Specialty) — *28% Model Weight*\n- Different departments and doctors have different average consultation speeds:\n  - **Emergency:** ~8 minutes per patient (fast triage).\n  - **General Medicine:** ~10 minutes per patient.\n  - **Pediatrics:** ~12 minutes per patient.\n  - **Orthopedics:** ~15 minutes per patient.\n  - **Cardiology:** ~18 minutes per patient (detailed ECG & history reviews).\n- If your token is in Cardiology, the wait per person ahead is higher than in Emergency.\n\n#### ③ `priority_code` (Triage Level: Normal, Medium, Emergency) — *14% Model Weight*\n- Standard queues treat everyone on a first-come, first-served basis, which is dangerous in a hospital.\n- **Emergency Priority:** The model drops the predicted wait time down to **2–4 minutes** because an emergency patient immediately bypasses the routine line.\n- **Medium Priority:** The model prioritizes them ahead of normal checkups (roughly 40% wait reduction).\n- **Normal Priority:** Processed in standard order.\n\n#### ④ `doctor_workload` (Fatigue & Administrative Overhead) — *8% Model Weight*\n- When a doctor has 3 patients waiting, they work quickly.\n- When a doctor has **16 to 20 patients waiting**, consultations slow down due to doctor fatigue, EHR documentation backlog, and patient coordination delays. The Random Forest model automatically applies a non-linear compounding penalty (+15% to +20%).\n\n#### ⑤ `doctor_available` (Doctor Status) — *4% Model Weight*\n- If the doctor is in their room: `doctor_available = 1`.\n- If the doctor is called to an emergency or takes a break (`doctor_available = 0`), the model factors in an additional standby buffer.\n\n#### ⑥ `arrival_hour` (Time-of-Day Congestion)\n- Peak morning hours (**10:00 AM – 12:00 PM**) and evening rush (**4:00 PM – 6:00 PM**) have higher hospital friction (pharmacy delays, lab sample transport) which the model incorporates from historical trends.\n\n#### ⑦ `delay_minutes` (Dynamic Cascade from Section 5)\n- If Doctor Sharma's current patient takes 25 minutes longer than expected (e.g., complex diagnosis), this accumulated delay is added directly to downstream predictions.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Step-by-Step Example Calculation\n\nSuppose a patient searches for **Token #103**:\n\n1. **Current Time:** `07:30 PM`\n2. **Assigned Doctor:** Dr. Rajesh Sharma (General Medicine, avg speed = `10 mins/patient`).\n3. **Queue Position:** 3 patients are ahead in line.\n4. **Priority:** Normal.\n5. **Doctor Backlog:** 14 patients waiting (high load).\n6. **Machine Learning Model Inference:**\n   $$\\text{Base Wait} \\approx 3 \\times 10\\text{ mins} = 30\\text{ mins}$$\n   $$\\text{High Backlog Overhead Penalty} \\approx +4\\text{ mins}$$\n   $$\\text{Total Predicted Wait Time} = \\mathbf{34\\text{ minutes}}$$\n7. **Expected Consultation Clock Calculation:**\n   $$\\text{Expected Clock} = \\text{07:30 PM} + 34\\text{ minutes} = \\mathbf{\\text{08:04 PM}}$$\n\n---\n\n### 5. What to Tell the Hackathon Evaluator (Talking Points)\n\n> *\"In traditional hospital displays, patients only see the token number currently being served. If Token #100 is inside and you are Token #105, you have no idea whether each patient will take 5 minutes or 30 minutes.*\n>\n> *In **Queue Oracle**, our Random Forest Regressor takes 7 live variables—including the doctor's average consultation speed, queue backlog, doctor availability, and triage priority—to give the patient both a **predicted waiting duration in minutes** and an **exact clock time** to expect their consultation. When delays happen, the expected time updates automatically.\"*","thinking":"Predicted waiting time signifies the anticipated time a user will spend waiting, measured in minutes, before receiving consultation. Expected consultation indicates the estimated duration, also in minutes, that a user's consultation is expected to last. Both times likely stem from historical data, current system load, and potentially user-specific factors.\n\n\nThe predicted wait time calculation combines several factors. It considers the number of patients ahead, the average consultation duration for the assigned doctor, and patient priority. Emergency cases have preferential treatment, directly impacting predicted wait times.\n\nDoctor workload introduces a non-linear component, where delays accumulate disproportionately as backlog increases due to increased overhead and potential fatigue. The doctor's availability and the current time of day are also considered. Accumulated delays from previous patients are added to arrive at the final predicted waiting time.\n\n\n"}
